<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belisarius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 500–<a href="/years/565">565</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Belisarius was the leading general of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>. He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> at Dara in 530, helped crush the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nika-riots">Nika riots</a> in 532, destroyed the Vandal Kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 533–534 and led the long reconquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>, taking Ravenna in 540. In old age he repelled the Kutrigur raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 559.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belisarius.jpg" alt="Belisarius" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petar Milošević · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belisarius_mosaic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Belisarius was born around 500, probably in Germaneia, a fortified town within the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and Paeonia. His ancestry is unknown. By 526 he was a senior guardsman of Justinian, nephew of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-i">Justin I</a>, though how he reached that post is unrecorded. In the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> his early record was mixed: he and Sittas raided Persian territory, but he lost his first independent command, fled with his troops at Tanurin after his colleagues were lured into a trap, and lost at Mindouos. He was promoted soon after, so he was probably not held to blame.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nika-riots">Nika riots</a> of 532, in which the Blues and Greens rose against Justinian and declared Hypatius emperor, Belisarius, Mundus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> were called in. Belisarius led his troops into the crowd in the Hippodrome while Narses blocked the other exits, and the revolt ended in a massacre in which between 30,000 and 60,000 people died, mostly unarmed civilians.</p><p>In June 533 Belisarius sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> with about 17,000 soldiers and a fleet, against the Vandal king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a>, who had deposed Justinian's guest friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilderic">Hilderic</a> in 530. He landed at Caput Vada rather than at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> at Ad Decimum and took Carthage, and Gelimer was compelled to surrender after Tricamarum. In 535 Justinian sent him against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, entering Syracuse on 31 December 535, and from March 537 to March 538 held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> against the far larger army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witigis">Vitiges</a>. By late 539 he had surrounded Ravenna.</p><p>In 559 Justinian called him from retirement to meet the Kutrigur Bulgars under Khan Zabergan, who had crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and approached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. With 300 veterans and a host of civilians he camped near them, had a trench dug and many torches lit to exaggerate his numbers, and placed his men in a narrow defile where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutrigurs">Kutrigurs</a> could not use their numbers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 562 Belisarius was tried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on a charge of conspiring against Justinian, found guilty and put under house arrest, but he was soon pardoned and restored to favour. He died in March 565. A later legend that he was blinded and reduced to begging near the Pincian Gate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> is thought apocryphal by most modern scholars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>'s Secret History, whose reliability is debated, portrays him as a cuckolded husband dependent on his wife Antonina, and recent work reads that portrait as invective. Historians generally rate him highly, chiefly for Dara, Ad Decimum and Tricamarum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Belisarius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 533–534 the Byzantine Empire under Justinian sent <b>Belisarius</b>, who defeated the Vandals and restored imperial rule; a province called Africa Proconsularis existed again within the Byzantine Praetorian Prefecture of Africa until the Arab conquest of Carthage in 698.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 533 <b>Belisarius</b> beat the Vandals at the Battle of Ad Decimum and the Battle of Tricamarum.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Iberian War, a Sasanian Persian army defeated the Byzantine army of <b>Belisarius</b> at Callinicum (Raqqa), in northern Syria, on Easter Saturday.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-19">19 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>West of Carthage, the Byzantine army of <b>Belisarius</b> defeated the Vandal king Gelimer, whose brother Tzazon was killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-15">15 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Having met Ostrogothic resistance only at Panormus, which fell after a quick siege, the Byzantine general <b>Belisarius</b> completed the conquest of Sicily and made a triumphal entry into Syracuse at the end of his consular year.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-31">31 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The citizens of Rome, mindful of the fate of Naples, put up no resistance to Justinian's general <b>Belisarius</b>, and the 4,000-strong Gothic garrison withdrew.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-9">9 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sapareva Banya, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.29,23.26&amp;year=542" aria-label="Sapareva Banya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5114 659 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Belisarius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5564 929h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5564 929h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.29,23.26&amp;year=542">What was here in 542 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…while human affairs follow whatever course may be in accordance with God's will, yet those who are in charge of any enterprise always win praise or blame according to their own deeds.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Justinian from besieged Rome, 537 (Procopius, History of the Wars, book 5, chapter 24) &#183; tr. H. B. Dewing, 1919 &#183; the letter as Procopius, his secretary, gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20298/pg20298-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For achievements which transcend the nature of things may not properly and fittingly be ascribed to man's valour, but to a stronger power.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Justinian from besieged Rome, 537 (Procopius, History of the Wars, book 5, chapter 24) &#183; tr. H. B. Dewing, 1919 &#183; the letter as Procopius, his secretary, gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20298/pg20298-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But I say to you that there will come a time when you will want to hide your heads under the thistles but will find no shelter anywhere.&#8221;</p><footer>Reply to the Gothic envoys at Rome, 537 (Procopius, History of the Wars, book 5, chapter 20) &#183; tr. H. B. Dewing, 1919 &#183; Procopius' account; he was Belisarius' secretary &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20298/pg20298-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;You, however, act as though you were playing at dice, and want to risk all on a single cast; but it is not my custom to choose the short course in preference to the advantageous one.&#8221;</p><footer>Words to the people of Rome, impatient for battle, 537 (Procopius, History of the Wars, book 5, chapter 28) &#183; tr. H. B. Dewing, 1919 &#183; Procopius' account; he was Belisarius' secretary &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20298/pg20298-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…we on our side permit the Goths to have the whole of Britain, which is much larger than Sicily and was subject to the Romans in early times.&#8221;</p><footer>Reply to the Gothic envoys during the siege of Rome, 537 (Procopius, History of the Wars, book 6, chapter 6) &#183; tr. H. B. Dewing, 1919 &#183; Procopius' account; the Goths had offered to give up Sicily &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20298/pg20298-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Although your promise gave us to understand that your words would be brief and temperate, yet your discourse has been both long and not far from fraudulent in its pretensions.&#8221;</p><footer>Reply to the Gothic envoys during the siege of Rome, 537 (Procopius, History of the Wars, book 6, chapter 6) &#183; tr. H. B. Dewing, 1919 &#183; Procopius' account; he was Belisarius' secretary &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20298/pg20298-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
